[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Tutorial: Mocking Void Methods in Mockito"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mocking methods that return void can be slightly different from mocking methods with return values because standard syntax like when(mock.method()).thenReturn(value) does not compile for void methods. This tutorial covers how to handle void methods effectively using Mockito."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before we begin, ensure you have Mockito included in your project dependencies. We will use the doNothing(), doThrow(), and doAnswer() families of methods to handle void return types."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Doing Nothing (Default Behavior)"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, Mockito already does nothing when a void method is called on a mock. However, you can explicitly state this using doNothing() for better readability or when you need to stub a void method to do nothing on consecutive calls."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"EmailService emailService = mock(EmailService.class);

// Explicitly configure the void method to do nothing
doNothing().when(emailService).sendWelcomeEmail("user@example.com");

// Calling the method will execute without throwing an exception
emailService.sendWelcomeEmail("user@example.com");"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Throwing Exceptions from Void Methods"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To test how your code handles failures in a void method, you can use doThrow() to force the void method to throw an exception when invoked."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"EmailService emailService = mock(EmailService.class);

// Configure the void method to throw an exception
doThrow(new RuntimeException("Server unavailable")).when(emailService).sendWelcomeEmail("user@example.com");

// This call will now throw the RuntimeException
emailService.sendWelcomeEmail("user@example.com");"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Executing Custom Logic with doAnswer"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes you need a void method to perform a side effect, such as modifying a parameter passed to it or capturing a callback. You can achieve this using doAnswer()."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"DatabaseService dbService = mock(DatabaseService.class);

doAnswer(invocation -> {
    String argument = invocation.getArgument(0);
    System.out.println("Saved: " + argument);
    return null; // void methods must return null in doAnswer
}).when(dbService).saveRecord(anyString());

dbService.saveRecord("Test Record");"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary of Best Practices"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use the doX() syntax (doNothing, doThrow, doAnswer) when working with void methods."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Remember that doAnswer() requires an explicit return null statement at the end of the lambda expression for void methods."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Combine void method mocking with verify() to ensure the method was actually called during the test execution."}]
